{"subjectId":"java","id":"concurrent-utilities","title":"ConcurrentHashMap, Atomics & Locks","group":"Concurrency","order":63,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>java.util.concurrent</code> replaces most hand-written <code>synchronized</code>/<code>wait</code> code with tested building blocks: concurrent collections, atomic variables, explicit locks and synchronizers.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Thread-safe maps compared</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>Hashtable</code></th><th><code>Collections.synchronizedMap</code></th><th><code>ConcurrentHashMap</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Locking</td><td>Every method locks the whole table</td><td>Every method locks one mutex</td><td>CAS + per-bin locks; reads are lock-free</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrent reads/writes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td><code>null</code> keys/values</td><td>Not allowed</td><td>Allowed (if the backing map allows)</td><td><strong>Not allowed</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Iteration while modifying</td><td>Fail-fast</td><td>Must lock manually; else CME</td><td>Weakly consistent, never throws CME</td></tr><tr><td>Atomic compound ops</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td><code>merge</code>, <code>compute</code>, <code>putIfAbsent</code>…</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Legacy</td><td>Simple wrapper</td><td>Default choice</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.List;\nimport java.util.TreeMap;\nimport java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        ConcurrentHashMap&lt;String, Integer&gt; counts = new ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();\n        List&lt;String&gt; words = List.of(\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"a\", \"b\");\n\n        Runnable countAll = () -&gt; { for (String w : words) counts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum); };  // atomic\n        Thread[] ts = new Thread[4];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; ts.length; i++) (ts[i] = new Thread(countAll)).start();\n        for (Thread t : ts) t.join();\n\n        System.out.println(new TreeMap&lt;&gt;(counts));     // sorted for display\n        counts.computeIfAbsent(\"d\", k -&gt; 0);\n        counts.compute(\"d\", (k, v) -&gt; v + 100);\n        System.out.println(\"d = \" + counts.get(\"d\") + \", size = \" + counts.size());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{a=12, b=8, c=4}\nd = 100, size = 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v);</code> is a check-then-act race even on a <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> — each call is atomic, the pair is not. Use <code>putIfAbsent</code>, <code>computeIfAbsent</code> or <code>merge</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-atom\"></i>Atomic variables and CAS</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>CAS (compare-and-swap)</strong> — a single CPU instruction: <em>if the value is still <code>expected</code>, set it to <code>new</code></em>. Atomic classes retry CAS in a loop, giving lock-free thread safety for single variables.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        AtomicInteger a = new AtomicInteger(5);\n        System.out.println(a.compareAndSet(5, 10) + \" \" + a.get());   // expected 5: swap\n        System.out.println(a.compareAndSet(5, 20) + \" \" + a.get());   // stale expectation: no swap\n        System.out.println(a.updateAndGet(x -&gt; x * 3));\n        System.out.println(a.getAndIncrement() + \" then \" + a.get());\n\n        AtomicInteger hits = new AtomicInteger();\n        LongAdder adder = new LongAdder();\n        Runnable work = () -&gt; {\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10_000; i++) { hits.incrementAndGet(); adder.increment(); }\n        };\n        Thread[] ts = new Thread[4];\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; ts.length; i++) (ts[i] = new Thread(work)).start();\n        for (Thread t : ts) t.join();\n        System.out.println(\"AtomicInteger = \" + hits.get() + \", LongAdder = \" + adder.sum());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true 10\nfalse 10\n30\n30 then 31\nAtomicInteger = 40000, LongAdder = 40000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>AtomicInteger</code> / <code>AtomicLong</code></td><td>Counters, sequence numbers, <code>compareAndSet</code> state machines</td></tr><tr><td><code>AtomicBoolean</code></td><td>Run-once flags (<code>if (started.compareAndSet(false, true))</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>AtomicReference&lt;V&gt;</code></td><td>Swap an immutable object atomically</td></tr><tr><td><code>LongAdder</code></td><td>Hot counters under heavy contention — cells per thread, summed on read</td></tr><tr><td><code>AtomicIntegerArray</code></td><td>Per-index atomic updates</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>ABA problem: CAS only checks the <em>value</em>, so A → B → A looks unchanged. When it matters (lock-free stacks), use <code>AtomicStampedReference</code>, which pairs the reference with a version stamp.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock-access\"></i>ReentrantLock and Condition</h3>\n<p>An explicit <code>Lock</code> does what <code>synchronized</code> does plus: try without blocking, time out, be interrupted while waiting, optional fairness, and several wait-sets (<code>Condition</code>s) per lock.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n        lock.lock();                                   // main holds it\n        try {\n            Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; System.out.println(\"worker tryLock: \" + lock.tryLock()));\n            t.start();\n            t.join();\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();                             // ALWAYS unlock in finally\n        }\n        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -&gt; {\n            if (lock.tryLock()) {\n                try { System.out.println(\"worker tryLock after unlock: true\"); }\n                finally { lock.unlock(); }\n            }\n        });\n        t2.start();\n        t2.join();\n        System.out.println(\"held now? \" + lock.isLocked());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>worker tryLock: false\nworker tryLock after unlock: true\nheld now? false</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java — Condition (compile-checked)</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\nimport java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;\nimport java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;\n\nclass LockBuffer&lt;T&gt; {\n    private final Deque&lt;T&gt; items = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n    private final int capacity;\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();    // two separate wait-sets\n    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();\n\n    LockBuffer(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }\n\n    void put(T x) throws InterruptedException {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            while (items.size() == capacity) notFull.await();\n            items.addLast(x);\n            notEmpty.signal();              // wake only a consumer\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n\n    T take() throws InterruptedException {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            while (items.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();\n            T x = items.removeFirst();\n            notFull.signal();               // wake only a producer\n            return x;\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>synchronized</code></th><th><code>ReentrantLock</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Release</td><td>Automatic at block end</td><td>Manual <code>unlock()</code> in <code>finally</code></td></tr><tr><td>Try / timeout</td><td>No</td><td><code>tryLock()</code>, <code>tryLock(t, unit)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Interruptible wait for lock</td><td>No</td><td><code>lockInterruptibly()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Fairness</td><td>No</td><td><code>new ReentrantLock(true)</code> (FIFO-ish, slower)</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions</td><td>One wait-set (<code>wait</code>/<code>notify</code>)</td><td>Many (<code>newCondition()</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Reentrant</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>ReentrantReadWriteLock</code> lets many readers in at once but writers alone — good for read-mostly data. <code>StampedLock</code> (Java 8) adds optimistic reads for even cheaper read paths.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-users-group\"></i>Synchronizers</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th><th>Reusable?</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>CountDownLatch(n)</code></td><td><code>await()</code> blocks until <code>countDown()</code> was called <code>n</code> times</td><td>No</td><td>Wait for N services to start / N tasks to finish</td></tr><tr><td><code>CyclicBarrier(n, action)</code></td><td><code>n</code> threads wait for each other, then all continue</td><td>Yes</td><td>Phased parallel algorithms</td></tr><tr><td><code>Semaphore(permits)</code></td><td><code>acquire()</code>/<code>release()</code> permits</td><td>Yes</td><td>Limit concurrency (max 10 DB connections)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Phaser</code></td><td>Flexible barrier with dynamic parties</td><td>Yes</td><td>Multi-phase work with join/leave</td></tr><tr><td><code>Exchanger</code></td><td>Two threads swap objects</td><td>Yes</td><td>Pipeline buffers</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.List;\nimport java.util.concurrent.*;\nimport java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {\n        // CountDownLatch: wait for 3 workers\n        CountDownLatch done = new CountDownLatch(3);\n        List&lt;String&gt; results = new CopyOnWriteArrayList&lt;&gt;();\n        for (String svc : List.of(\"db\", \"cache\", \"queue\")) {\n            new Thread(() -&gt; { results.add(svc + \" ready\"); done.countDown(); }).start();\n        }\n        done.await();\n        System.out.println(results.stream().sorted().toList());\n\n        // Semaphore: at most 2 tasks inside at once\n        Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(2);\n        AtomicInteger inside = new AtomicInteger(), maxInside = new AtomicInteger();\n        try (ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(6)) {\n            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++) {\n                pool.submit(() -&gt; {\n                    permits.acquire();\n                    try {\n                        maxInside.accumulateAndGet(inside.incrementAndGet(), Math::max);\n                        Thread.sleep(20);\n                        inside.decrementAndGet();\n                    } finally {\n                        permits.release();\n                    }\n                    return null;\n                });\n            }\n        }\n        System.out.println(\"max concurrent &lt;= 2: \" + (maxInside.get() &lt;= 2));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[cache ready, db ready, queue ready]\nmax concurrent &lt;= 2: true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Other concurrent collections: <code>CopyOnWriteArrayList</code> (copy on every write — ideal for rarely-changed listener lists), <code>ConcurrentLinkedQueue</code> (lock-free queue), <code>ConcurrentSkipListMap</code>/<code>Set</code> (sorted, concurrent <code>TreeMap</code>/<code>TreeSet</code>).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How does ConcurrentHashMap achieve thread safety (Java 8+)?</summary><div><p>The table is an array of bins. Inserting into an empty bin uses CAS; otherwise the thread locks only that bin's first node. Reads are lock-free via volatile reads. Long bins turn into red-black trees. Resizing is done cooperatively by several threads. (Java 7 used 16 lock <em>segments</em> instead.)</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why doesn't ConcurrentHashMap allow null?</summary><div><p>Ambiguity: <code>get(k)</code> returning <code>null</code> could mean \"absent\" or \"mapped to null\", and unlike a single-threaded map you can't follow up with <code>containsKey</code> atomically — another thread may change the map in between.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>AtomicInteger vs LongAdder?</summary><div><p><code>AtomicInteger</code> is one CAS'd variable — exact reads, but many threads hammering it retry a lot. <code>LongAdder</code> spreads increments over per-thread cells and sums on <code>sum()</code>, scaling far better for write-heavy counters, at the cost of a non-atomic snapshot read.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>CountDownLatch vs CyclicBarrier?</summary><div><p>A latch is one-shot: some threads count down, others await; it cannot be reset. A barrier makes a fixed group of threads wait for <em>each other</em>, can run a barrier action, and resets automatically for the next round.</p></div></details></section>"}